### Inconsistencies found — reported, not silently changed

1. **§F.1's G4 "If it fails" cell is stale.** It reads *"Drop to UC-1 and UC-5, and say so"*, but neither UC-1 nor UC-5 is in the committed set (`UC-1, UC-3, UC-4, UC-6`), and §B.3 C6's stated cut is the tier-based set `UC-1, UC-3, UC-5, UC-6`. **A judgement call, so left untouched and reported:** the cell may mean the tier-based cut (in which case UC-1 and UC-3 should also be named) or a use-case cut no longer in the design. **I believe §F.1 is the wrong document here:** §B.3 C6 is the normative statement of both reductions and §F.1 should point at it.
2. **§F.1's G3 gate definition omits the week number** *("A working local model calls a toy MCP tool …")* while its Week column says 8 and every other gate row names its week inline. Cosmetic; **not changed**, because rewording a gate's condition text risks the emphasis parser for no substantive gain.
3. **§C.6's Stage D row and §F.3's Stage D prose say *"weeks 10–12"* while its own label reads *"weeks 10–11 that is never an exit criterion"*.** §F.3's prose now states the weeks 10–12 window (§C.6's) **and** notes the bar covers weeks 10–12. The residual mismatch is inside §F.3's own sentence, where the original modifier *"in weeks 10–11"* came from the previous 2-week bar. **Judgement call:** the substantive claim (*"never an exit criterion"*) is unaffected and the required *"weeks 10–12"* statement is now present, so the residual phrase is reported rather than rewritten again.
4. **A relative-ordering subtlety, reported for completeness.** §F.2 shows UC-1/UC-3 implementation in **week 9**, while the first band draws `UC-1 / UC-3` at weeks 7–8 and `UC-2 / UC-4 / UC-5` at weeks 8–9. This is the original chart's relative ordering carried forward (each row one week later than the last, offset from §F.2's rows by construction); §F.2 is unaffected and §C.6's coverage windows are honoured. Not changed.

**Nothing outside §F.3 and the one §C.6 Stage C cell was edited, and every change is confined to the chart, its three prose paragraphs, and that cell.**

